# METHOD CLAIM

**Claim 1.** A method for characterizing subsurface rock permeability across a plurality of locations within a geologic formation, the method comprising:

(a) acquiring seismic survey data representative of a subsurface region encompassing the plurality of locations, wherein the seismic survey data comprises compressional wave velocity data and shear wave velocity data;

(b) obtaining well log data from at least one borehole penetrating the subsurface region, the well log data comprising lithological data, porosity data, mineral composition data, and fluid saturation data;

(c) processing the seismic survey data to generate a three-dimensional seismic velocity model of the subsurface region, the velocity model comprising compressional wave velocity values and shear wave velocity values at each of the plurality of locations;

(d) classifying, using the lithological data and the well log data, each of the plurality of locations into one of a plurality of predefined lithological categories, wherein the plurality of predefined lithological categories comprises at least a sandstone category, a carbonate category, and a shale category;

(e) for each of the plurality of locations, selecting a rock physics model from a plurality of rock physics models based on the lithological category assigned in step (d), wherein each rock physics model comprises a stiff pore-space model component and a compliant pore-space model component;

(f) computing, for each of the plurality of locations, a stiff porosity fraction and a compliant porosity fraction by:

   (i) calculating a dry rock bulk modulus and a dry rock shear modulus from the compressional wave velocity value and the shear wave velocity value at said location using a Gassmann fluid substitution relation and the fluid saturation data;

   (ii) applying the stiff pore-space model component to compute a stiff pore aspect ratio and a corresponding stiff porosity contribution, wherein the stiff pore-space model component represents pores having an aspect ratio greater than a predetermined threshold value;

   (iii) applying the compliant pore-space model component to compute a compliant pore aspect ratio and a corresponding compliant porosity contribution, wherein the compliant pore-space model component represents pores having an aspect ratio less than or equal to the predetermined threshold value; and

   (iv) determining a total effective porosity at said location as a combination of the stiff porosity contribution and the compliant porosity contribution;

(g) computing, for each of the plurality of locations, an estimated permeability value from the stiff porosity fraction, the compliant porosity fraction, and the lithological category assigned in step (d), using a permeability-porosity transform derived from the selected rock physics model, wherein the permeability-porosity transform assigns a first weighting factor to the stiff porosity fraction and a second weighting factor, different from the first weighting factor, to the compliant porosity fraction;

(h) generating a permeability map or volume representing the estimated permeability value at each of the plurality of locations across the subsurface region; and

(i) outputting the permeability map or volume to a display device or storage medium for use in evaluating hydrocarbon production potential or fluid flow characteristics of the geologic formation.

---

**Claim 2.** The method of claim 1, wherein the predetermined threshold value for the pore aspect ratio is in a range of 0.01 to 0.1.

**Claim 3.** The method of claim 1, wherein step (f)(i) further comprises correcting the dry rock bulk modulus and dry rock shear modulus for effective pressure using a pressure log or an estimated overburden stress value.

**Claim 4.** The method of claim 1, wherein the stiff pore-space model component comprises a Kuster-Toksöz inclusion model, and the compliant pore-space model component comprises a modified Hertz-Mindlin contact model.

**Claim 5.** The method of claim 1, wherein the first weighting factor applied to the stiff porosity fraction in step (g) is greater than the second weighting factor applied to the compliant porosity fraction, reflecting a greater contribution of stiff, well-connected pore space to fluid flow relative to compliant, crack-like pore space.

**Claim 6.** The method of claim 1, wherein classifying each of the plurality of locations in step (d) further comprises applying a machine learning classifier trained on the lithological data and the well log data to assign a probability distribution across the plurality of predefined lithological categories, and selecting the lithological category having a highest probability value.

**Claim 7.** The method of claim 1, wherein the permeability-porosity transform in step (g) is calibrated using core sample permeability measurements obtained from the at least one borehole.

**Claim 8.** The method of claim 1, further comprising iteratively updating the rock physics model selected in step (e) by comparing the estimated permeability value to a measured permeability value obtained from a formation test, and adjusting at least one parameter of the stiff pore-space model component or the compliant pore-space model component to minimize a difference between the estimated permeability value and the measured permeability value.

**Claim 9.** The method of claim 1, wherein the fluid saturation data comprises water saturation, oil saturation, and gas saturation, and wherein step (f)(i) further comprises computing an effective fluid bulk modulus using a Reuss average or a patchy saturation model prior to applying the Gassmann fluid substitution relation.

**Claim 10.** The method of claim 1, further comprising identifying, based on the permeability map or volume, at least one candidate location for well placement or hydraulic fracturing, and outputting the at least one candidate location.
